00:07:31 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: It’s completely understandable to have strong feelings about who accompanies children on a school trip. When approaching the school, the most effective and constructive way is to frame your request around general safeguarding principles and your own comfort as a parent, rather than making it about one individual in a way that could sound accusatory or discriminatory without specific cause. Here’s a calm, respectful way to phrase it – you can adapt this for a conversation or an email: --- **Suggested wording (email or in-person summary):** > Dear [Headteacher / Trip Organiser], > > I’m writing about the upcoming school trip to [destination]. I want to raise a sensitive but important point about supervision. > > I understand that Father [Name] is planned to attend as a helper. While I respect his role in the parish, I have personal reservations about a member of the clergy, who is not a trained teacher or regular school staff member, having unsupervised access to children on a school outing. Given the wider national context around safeguarding and the Church, I would feel more comfortable if the school’s supervision relied solely on vetted, DBS-checked school staff and parent volunteers. > > Could you please clarify what the adult-to-child ratios will be, who exactly will be supervising, and what safeguarding measures are in place for any non-school adults? I would be grateful if you could reconsider Father [Name]’s involvement, or at least ensure that he is not in a position of sole responsibility. > > I’m raising this purely out of a desire for full reassurance about my child’s welfare, and I hope you can understand my perspective. > > Thank you for your time. --- ### Why this approach works: - **It avoids a personal attack.** You’re not saying *“I don’t trust this priest”* without evidence; you’re questioning the *role* of someone not employed by the school. - **It shifts the focus to policy.** Schools have a duty of care and must follow strict safeguarding rules. Asking about ratios, vetting, and supervision forces them to justify the arrangement. - **It gives them a way out.** The school can quietly decide not to include the priest without it becoming a public confrontation. - **It leaves the door open for an alternative.** For example, they might confirm the priest is only there for a short blessing and will never be alone with children – which might address your concern. ### If you have a specific, concrete concern. If you’re aware of actual misconduct, inappropriate behaviour, or a specific risk, then this is not about phrasing – it’s a safeguarding matter. In that case, speak directly and confidentially to the school’s Designated Safeguarding Lead (DSL) or the headteacher, stating facts clearly. The school has a legal duty to investigate and act. Otherwise, voicing a general “I’d rather he wasn’t there” without a safeguarding reason may be heard as prejudice, and you’re right to think carefully about how you phrase it. The wording above protects your child’s welfare while respecting the need for a balanced, non-confrontational conversation.
