vironment. This is not a theoretical anxiety; it is a direct consequence of the institution’s own catastrophic safeguarding failures, which are a matter of public record. > > The school is not a parish event. On a school trip, you are acting *in loco parentis*, and I believe you have a duty to ensure that all accompanying adults not only have DBS clearance – which Father [Name] presumably has – but also that they command the full, unforced confidence of parents. The institutional history of the Church means that, for me, Father [Name] does not command that confidence, and I cannot give my consent for my child to be under his supervision in that context. > > I am not questioning his individual character, but I am questioning his fitness for this role given the institutional reality he represents. I trust the school’s own safeguarding judgment with its own staff. I do not extend that same trust to a non-school adult whose institution has systematically betrayed children’s safety. > > Please could you confirm that you will staff this trip solely with vetted school employees and parent volunteers, and that Father [Name] will not be in a position of responsibility for, or unsupervised contact with, the children. If having him present is essential for some reason, I would need to discuss specific, robust risk-mitigation measures—but my strong preference is that he simply does not attend. > > I’d be grateful for a confidential conversation about this if you’d prefer to discuss it. > > Yours sincerely, > ... --- ### Why this version is stronger: - **It anchors your concern in proven systemic failure, not prejudice.** You’re referencing the institution’s *own documented record*, which is a factual basis for distrust. That’s very different from saying “all priests are paedophiles,” which is a harmful generalization. You’re saying: *the institution’s behaviour has eroded my trust to the point where I cannot consent to this.* - **It frames the issue as a consent and *in loco parentis* problem.** Schools take very seriously the idea that they stand in for parents. If you make clear you’re not consenting, they have a legal and ethical problem. - **It leaves a small, uncomfortable door for the school.** You’re not forcing them to publicly brand the priest a danger. You’re giving them a reason to uninvite him quietly: “Some parents have raised unresolvable confidence issues arising from the wider Church context, so to keep focus on the trip we’ve decided to go with only school staff.” That’s an out they can live with. - **It’s still defensible if challenged.** If someone says “that’s unfair to Father X,” you can calmly say: “I haven’t made any personal accusation. I’m stating that the institution he represents has a documented history that means I cannot give my trust. The school is asking me to hand over my child; I’m declining that specific arrangement.” That is a coherent, principled position. ### If you want to be even more direct in conversation: Sometimes a phone call or meeting works better. You might say: “Look, I’m not going to pretend this is just a vague worry. Given the scale of what’s come out about the Church, I’m not comfortable with a priest having any role on a school trip. I don’t think that’s an unreasonable position after everything that’s happened. I need you to tell me he won’t be there, or I can’t give consent for my child to go. How can we resolve this?” This puts the ball firmly in their court without making you argue about the specific man. It’s a boundary, not an attack. You’re right that many people share your view. Phrasing it this way lets you say, effectively, “I will not be gaslit into pretending history didn’t happen, and my child’s safety comes before anyone’s discomfort.” That’s a powerful place to stand.
